From 2cf6e4ee5d8dff3fa5a3a507615da53f92681402 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 01:51:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ci(publish): a go target, so a Go release can be dispatched

Go has no registry, so its release is the go/v<version> tag alone, and
until now only `make publish-go` on a workstation could write it.
`target: go` runs the same steps in CI. verify-go (contents: read) runs
make build, make vet, whose first pass has no workspace, and make test,
the library's tests and the corpus. tag-go (contents: write) then writes
the annotated tag go/v<go/VERSION>, as go/Makefile does.

The guards match the other targets: a dispatch must come from main,
expect_sha must match, and a tag already on another commit is refused.
dry_run runs everything and writes no tag. There is no push trigger for
go/v*, since a pushed Go tag is already the release.

AGENTS.md says the route exists.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0167vhgRnddZXZJm3gcM7pwk
---
 .github/workflows/publish.yml | 163 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 AGENTS.md                     |   9 +-
 2 files changed, 163 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/publish.yml b/.github/workflows/publish.yml
index 96f19c0..fe252cc 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/publish.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/publish.yml
@@ -1,7 +1,8 @@
 # Releases the three voxgig/struct packages that publish over OIDC, and
 # writes their git tags: `@voxgig/struct` on npm (from `typescript/`),
 # `@voxgig/struct-js` on npm (from `javascript/`), and `voxgig-struct` on
-# crates.io (from `rust/`).
+# crates.io (from `rust/`). It also releases the Go module (from `go/`),
+# which has no registry: its release is the tag alone. See GO below.
 #
 # Adapted from voxgig/sdkgen's publish.yml, which carries the reasoning for
 # every guard below. BOTH registries' trusted publishers are registered
@@ -24,12 +25,11 @@
 # fails earlier and more plainly: the token exchange in
 # `rust-lang/crates-io-auth-action` fails and `cargo publish` never runs.
 #
-# ONE TARGET PER RUN. The three packages version independently (typescript
-# is 0.3.x, javascript and rust are 0.1.x) and every publish is
-# irreversible, so a dispatch releases exactly ONE of them.
+# ONE TARGET PER RUN. The four ports version independently and every
+# release is irreversible, so a dispatch releases exactly ONE of them.
 #
 # TARGETS ARE NAMED BY PORT, not by registry: `typescript`, `javascript`,
-# `rust`. Two of the three publish to npm, so the old `npm` name stopped
+# `rust`, `go`. Two of them publish to npm, so the old `npm` name stopped
 # identifying anything the moment the second one arrived. That is also how
 # the rest of the repository names them -- PUBLISH_LANGS, `make
 # publish-<lang>`, and the `<lang>/v*` tags.
@@ -40,7 +40,9 @@
 #                          id-token: write, contents: READ. Run the install,
 #                          the build and the tests — i.e. dependency lifecycle
 #                          scripts and project code.
-#   tag, tag-javascript, tag-rust
+#   verify-go              contents: READ. The build and the tests. Go has
+#                          no registry, so nothing here needs id-token.
+#   tag, tag-javascript, tag-rust, tag-go
 #                          contents: write. Run git and nothing else.
 #
 # Keeping them in one job would hand a repository-write credential to every
@@ -62,7 +64,9 @@
 #   push: tags rust/v*       the same, for the crate.
 #
 # A `*` in a ref filter does not cross `/`, so the three push paths never
-# overlap. The tag jobs do not run on a push.
+# overlap. The tag jobs do not run on a push. go has no push path: a
+# `go/v*` tag pushed by hand is already the release, and leaves a run on it
+# nothing to do.
 #
 # EVERY PORT TAGS AS `<lang>/v<version>`, TYPESCRIPT INCLUDED. It released
 # under a BARE `v<version>` up to 0.3.4, and the 16 bare tags in this repo are
@@ -101,6 +105,16 @@
 # breath. Weigh that against the alternative, which is that the trusted
 # publisher can only ever be tested by an irreversible release.
 #
+# GO HAS NO REGISTRY, so `target: go` is `make publish-go` run here rather
+# than on a workstation. verify-go runs the build, `vet` with no workspace
+# (as a consumer resolves the module) and the tests and corpus that
+# `go/Makefile`'s `test` runs; tag-go then writes `go/v<version>`, annotated
+# as the Makefile writes it. That tag is PERMANENT: proxy.golang.org and
+# sum.golang.org cache a version immutably, and moving or deleting the tag
+# reaches users as a checksum error, so a bad release is withdrawn only by
+# `retract` in a later one. A dry run runs every guard and test and writes
+# no tag.
+#
 # VERSION BUMPS ARE NOT AUTOMATED, deliberately. Nothing here commits. It
 # reads the version already on the branch and releases exactly that, so the
 # bump stays a reviewable diff and the release stays a button.
@@ -108,6 +122,7 @@
 #   typescript/package.json "version" -> tag typescript/v<version>
 #   javascript/package.json "version" -> tag javascript/v<version>
 #   rust/Cargo.toml         version   -> tag rust/v<version>
+#   go/VERSION                        -> tag go/v<version>
 #
 # Renaming this file breaks publishing until BOTH registries' trusted
 # publishers are updated to match. Do NOT publish locally over a token: that
@@ -141,7 +156,7 @@ on:
       target:
         description: 'Which port to release'
         type: choice
-        options: [typescript, javascript, rust]
+        options: [typescript, javascript, rust, go]
         default: typescript
       expect_sha:
         description: 'Optional: refuse unless main is still at this commit (set by `make publish`)'
@@ -929,3 +944,135 @@ jobs:
           git tag "$TAG"
           git push origin "refs/tags/$TAG"
           echo "pushed $TAG" >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
+
+  # Go has no registry, so this pair is `make publish-go` in CI: verify-go
+  # runs what that target runs before it tags, and tag-go writes the tag.
+  # See GO in the header.
+  verify-go:
+    name: go verify
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    timeout-minutes: 20
+
+    if: ${{ github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' && inputs.target == 'go' }}
+
+    permissions:
+      contents: read
+
+    outputs:
+      version: ${{ steps.versions.outputs.version }}
+
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7
+        with:
+          fetch-depth: 0
+
+      - name: Setup Go
+        uses: actions/setup-go@b7ad1dad31e06c5925ef5d2fc7ad053ef454303e # v7
+        with:
+          go-version-file: go/go.mod
+
+      # THE SAME FILE `go/Makefile` READS: Go has no manifest version field.
+      - name: Resolve the version being released
+        id: versions
+        working-directory: go
+        run: |
+          set -euo pipefail
+          VERSION="$(cat VERSION)"
+          test -n "$VERSION" || { echo "::error::go/VERSION is empty"; exit 1; }
+          echo "version=$VERSION" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
+          echo "$VERSION -> tag go/v$VERSION"
+
+      - name: Dispatches must come from main
+        if: ${{ !inputs.dry_run }}
+        run: |
+          set -euo pipefail
+          if [ "$GITHUB_REF_NAME" != "main" ]; then
+            echo "::error::dispatch ref is $GITHUB_REF_NAME; releases must come from main"
+            exit 1
+          fi
+
+      - name: main must still be the commit we intended to release
+        if: ${{ inputs.expect_sha != '' }}
+        env:
+          EXPECT_SHA: ${{ inputs.expect_sha }}
+        run: |
+          set -euo pipefail
+          if [ "$GITHUB_SHA" != "$EXPECT_SHA" ]; then
+            echo "::error::main has moved: expected $EXPECT_SHA, got $GITHUB_SHA"
+            exit 1
+          fi
+
+      # Warns rather than stops on a rehearsal — see the npm job's copy of
+      # this step for why.
+      - name: Refuse a tag that points at a different commit
+        env:
+          GO_VERSION: ${{ steps.versions.outputs.version }}
+          DRY_RUN: ${{ inputs.dry_run }}
+        run: |
+          set -euo pipefail
+          TAG="go/v$GO_VERSION"
+          HEAD_SHA="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
+          AT="$(git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/$TAG^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || true)"
+          if [ -n "$AT" ] && [ "$AT" != "$HEAD_SHA" ]; then
+            MSG="tag $TAG already exists on $AT, not this commit — bump go/VERSION"
+            if [ "${DRY_RUN:-false}" = "true" ]; then
+              echo "::warning::$MSG (rehearsal: a real release stops here)"
+            else
+              echo "::error::$MSG"
+              exit 1
+            fi
+          fi
+          if [ -n "$AT" ]; then echo "$TAG already points here; nothing to create"; fi
+
+      # `vet` first runs with no workspace, as a consumer resolves the module,
+      # so an import from outside it fails here rather than in a dependent's
+      # build. `test` is what `make publish-go` runs before it tags: the
+      # library's tests, and the corpus in ./testutil with omni from its tag.
+      - name: Build, vet and test
+        working-directory: go
+        run: |
+          make build
+          make vet
+          make test
+
+  # Runs git and nothing else — see the `tag` job above, which this mirrors.
+  tag-go:
+    name: tag (go)
+    needs: verify-go
+    if: ${{ github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' && inputs.target == 'go' && !inputs.dry_run }}
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    timeout-minutes: 10
+
+    permissions:
+      contents: write
+
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7
+        with:
+          fetch-depth: 0
+
+      - name: Tag the release
+        env:
+          GO_VERSION: ${{ needs.verify-go.outputs.version }}
+        run: |
+          set -euo pipefail
+          TAG="go/v$GO_VERSION"
+          HEAD_SHA="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
+
+          # EXISTENCE IS NOT ENOUGH — dereference and compare, for the reason
+          # spelled out in the npm tag job.
+          AT="$(git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/$TAG^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || true)"
+          if [ -n "$AT" ]; then
+            if [ "$AT" != "$HEAD_SHA" ]; then
+              echo "::error::$TAG exists on $AT, not this commit ($HEAD_SHA)"
+              exit 1
+            fi
+            echo "$TAG already points here — nothing to do" >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
+            exit 0
+          fi
+          # Annotated, as `go/Makefile` writes it, so every Go tag is one kind.
+          git -c user.name='github-actions[bot]' \
+              -c user.email='41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com' \
+              tag -a "$TAG" -m "go v$GO_VERSION"
+          git push origin "refs/tags/$TAG"
+          echo "pushed $TAG" >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
diff --git a/AGENTS.md b/AGENTS.md
index b08ea9a..2259ced 100644
--- a/AGENTS.md
+++ b/AGENTS.md
@@ -357,6 +357,12 @@ other port uses; only the credential and the operator changed. Typescript
 and rust have a trusted publisher registered against `publish.yml`;
 javascript does not yet, which is the paragraph after next.
 
+**go can be released from CI too**, by a `target: go` dispatch. It runs the
+build, `vet` and the tests `make publish-go` runs, then writes the same
+annotated `go/v<version>` tag. Go has no registry, so no credential is
+involved and the tag is the whole release; the dispatch exists so that a Go
+release needs no workstation. `make publish-go` still works.
+
 Typescript joined that shape late. It released under a bare `v<version>`
 up to `v0.3.4`, and those 16 tags stay — they are what those releases went
 out under. Everything from the next release on is `typescript/v<version>`,
@@ -446,7 +452,8 @@ know what it does and does not do:
 ### The three OIDC ports go through CI, not the Makefile
 
 **Actions → publish → Run workflow** on `main`, picking `target:
-typescript`, `javascript` or `rust`; or push the matching tag by hand
+typescript`, `javascript` or `rust` (or `go`, for a tag-only Go release);
+or push the matching tag by hand
 (`typescript/v<version>`, `javascript/v<version>`, `rust/v<version>`). One
 target per run: the three packages version independently and every publish is
 irreversible. `make publish-all` drives exactly this tag-push path for all
